1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:02,370
It is Friday at Harbor Learning Circle.

2
00:00:02,730 --> 00:00:04,860
Morgan opens a paper brief, not a live

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:05,720
dashboard.

4
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:07,940
The preparation agent's job was to

5
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:10,500
compare three cleared course inputs and

6
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:12,190
draft a Session Option Brief.

7
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,230
The first line says prepared, not sent.

8
00:00:15,606 --> 00:00:17,995
A second line says staffing is unconfirmed.

9
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:20,939
A third says a research proposal has not run.

10
00:00:21,878 --> 00:00:24,310
Morgan's day is not about clearing every row.

11
00:00:24,651 --> 00:00:28,278
It is about spending judgment where a decision can change what happens next.

12
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:30,403
The Six-Beat Day begins with a brief.

13
00:00:30,915 --> 00:00:31,342
What was prepared?

14
00:00:31,982 --> 00:00:32,622
What needs a human?

15
00:00:33,006 --> 00:00:33,902
What may be worth learning?

16
00:00:34,713 --> 00:00:41,881
Harbor's answer is short: one paper brief was drafted; staffing and spend need decisions; a layout question may deserve a test.

17
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:45,008
The brief must carry receipts and uncertainty.

18
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:50,128
If it reads like an endless task list, the system has handed burden back to Morgan.

19
00:00:51,109 --> 00:00:52,176
Second, route.

20
00:00:52,755 --> 00:00:57,577
The staffing question belongs to the program lead and team, not to the preparation agent.

21
00:00:57,961 --> 00:01:00,777
A proposed spend belongs in a separate budget decision.

22
00:01:01,417 --> 00:01:04,873
The research question belongs to Morgan as owner of the brief format.

23
00:01:05,318 --> 00:01:10,097
Route each item to a named person, with a deadline or review window where appropriate.

24
00:01:10,438 --> 00:01:13,980
Routing an item to a queue without an owner is a way to lose it politely.

25
00:01:14,662 --> 00:01:15,430
Third, review.

26
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:19,713
Morgan reads the brief beside its source-status summary and trace template.

27
00:01:20,097 --> 00:01:22,657
One course source is stale for the proposed month.

28
00:01:23,211 --> 00:01:25,131
The agent's draft sounds confident anyway.

29
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,509
Morgan marks the claim unsupported and keeps the staffing hold in place.

30
00:01:30,149 --> 00:01:31,173
Fourth, approve.

31
00:01:31,770 --> 00:01:34,117
There is nothing to approve for a team proposal today.

32
00:01:34,711 --> 00:01:42,647
Morgan may approve a correction to the paper draft for later review, but that is not approval to book a session, spend money, or send an announcement.

33
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:44,397
Fifth, recover.

34
00:01:45,029 --> 00:01:49,381
Suppose the paper handoff is incomplete: the draft lacks its source-status field.

35
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:51,899
Morgan does not fill the gap by guessing.

36
00:01:52,566 --> 00:01:58,795
The recovery choice is to return the brief for a corrected paper draft, preserve the earlier version, and record why.

37
00:01:59,349 --> 00:02:01,781
There is no automatic retry or provider call.

38
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:03,702
Sixth, learn.

39
00:02:04,358 --> 00:02:10,076
The repeated missing source-status field could become a memory proposal: require that field in future briefs.

40
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:14,812
It stays a proposal until Morgan reviews its source, scope, and expiry.

41
00:02:15,068 --> 00:02:17,798
One bad draft does not write policy by itself.

42
00:02:18,578 --> 00:02:24,423
At day's end, Morgan asks one uncomfortable question: what did I approve that I should have questioned?

43
00:02:24,807 --> 00:02:28,605
In this rehearsal the answer is a paper correction, and Morgan can revisit it.

44
00:02:29,133 --> 00:02:33,826
The staffing, spend, research, and memory decisions remain distinct.

45
00:02:34,381 --> 00:02:39,031
Pause and name the beat in your workflow where a human is most likely to approve too quickly.

46
00:02:39,671 --> 00:02:41,933
Write a Six-Beat Day brief for your workflow.

47
00:02:42,509 --> 00:02:47,970
For each beat, name the item, evidence, owner, safe action, and stop condition.

48
00:02:48,525 --> 00:02:49,677
Make one thing fail.

49
00:02:50,146 --> 00:02:53,133
Show how the operator recovers without hiding the original version.

50
00:02:53,748 --> 00:02:57,204
Mark any lesson as a memory proposal rather than an approved rule.

51
00:02:57,887 --> 00:03:00,106
Close with the question you would ask after a busy day.

52
00:03:00,746 --> 00:03:02,538
Use a fictional or approved example.

53
00:03:03,257 --> 00:03:53,932
Do not use private operational data, open a live account, import a trace, or contact a person.

54
00:03:54,444 --> 00:03:56,151
Save revision fifteen as a draft.

55
00:03:56,706 --> 00:04:02,124
Harbor's day ends with a held brief, a visible correction path, and decisions still owned by people.

56
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:04,534
The six beats are useful because they

57
00:04:04,574 --> 00:04:06,324
make those boundaries routine.
